body:
Notice you pass `nested.objectControlled` as a controlled input field name to your controlled component into the `useController` hook.
```js
 <NestedControlledField control={control} name="nested.objectControlled" />
```
The field you're updating with the `setValue` method is `nested`
```js
setValue("nested", {
  objectControlled: {
    nestedValue: "updated"
  }
});
```
which is not the field your controlled input is being subscribed to. Remember, you've passed `name="nested.objectControlled"` not `name="nested"`. Hence, your controlled field value is not being updated / re-rendered.

So, the **not working** behavior is expect. In your case, you can
- set your controlled input name as `nested` to track the whole object
- use `setValue` with the exact or deeper nested field name
```js
setValue("nested.objectControlled", {
  nestedValue: "updated"
});
```
or
```js
setValue("nested.objectControlled.nestedValue", "updated");
```
See [this CSB](https://codesandbox.io/s/react-hook-form-object-controlled-not-updated-forked-40e1c?file=/src/index.js) for example

Hm, it's an interesting point, tho.
From [the `useController` source code](https://github.com/react-hook-form/react-hook-form/blob/master/src/useController.ts#L53) _(I've omitted the irrelevant code bits for the ease of scan)_
```js
function useController() {
  const isArrayField = isNameInFieldArray(control._names.array, name);
  const value = useWatch({
    exact: !isArrayField,
  })
}
```
So, that implies that for field arrays we're watching the whole array object and for an object field input we're subscribing to the exact field `name` value set. Is it correct, @bluebill1049? Is it a performance thing?
